module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.generic_match_field_binding_keeps_its_type_parameter

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { BlockerClassUnstated, RecurringFailureMode, RungUnread }

data generic_match_field_binding_keeps_its_type_parameter: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "generic_match_field_binding_keeps_its_type_parameter" as NonEmptyStr,

  rung: RungUnread { cause: BlockerClassUnstated },

  receipts: [
    "SPECIMEN CLASS, 2026-11-15 (merry-fox-617, taking over crisp-dove-701, PR 16520; node native-emit-match-binding-keeps-its-type; purpose v2 self-host/native closure qualification under gunbc.v1.v1_maintenance_standing::v1_seed_standing). A `match` that binds a variant FIELD out of a GENERIC enum and crosses that binding with `as` lowered the binding to compile_error!(UNRESOLVED_CompilerError) at NATIVE EMIT, while typecheck and interpretation both ACCEPTED the program. Minimal reproduction: `fn use_c(p: Optional<NonEmptyStr>) -> String { match p { Absent {} => \"none\"  Present { value: v } => v as String } }`. The bound field's resolved type still named the enum's type parameter T instead of the scrutinee's concrete argument, so the emitter (whose read-node.inferred contract is correct and was NOT special-cased) had no type to cross and emitted the refusal. A non-generic enum with the identical binding shape emitted correctly throughout, which pins the defect to generic-enum field bindings and excludes arm order, fn-return and match binding in general.",
    "EARLIEST UNJUSTIFIED BOUNDARY (gunbc.design_argument::earliest_unjustified_boundary, gunbc.design_argument::single_authority): the kernel MINT of the generic coproduct, v1.compiler.infer_types kernel_optional_type_node, is a hand-built second spelling of `type Optional<T> = Absent | Present { value: T }`. It was spelled with params: [] and Present.value.inferred = TypeVariable { present_value }, decoupled from the declaration. Because the mint carried no parameter, v1.compiler.infer_patterns generic_use_slot_bindings built an EMPTY slot map at every use site, so the matched Present.value field kept its unbound payload node and was never rewritten to the concrete argument. The mint was the one node violating the shape that v1.compiler.infer_types kernel_optional_shape_refusal already ENFORCES of every Optional declaration (params count 1, payload field typed by the parameter). The defect surfaced only at emit because typecheck and interpretation never read the static field type; the emitter does. It could not be DERIVED from the std.optional declaration: the kernel type environment is the seed that typechecks std.optional itself, so reading that declaration is circular. Repair, part one: the mint carries the declaration's parameter -- the T parameter is in params and Present.value is typed by it -- so generic_use_slot_bindings resolves the slot. Repair, part two (reworked under review 95641): NOTHING is added in v1.compiler.infer_patterns. The first attempt added a parallel path there -- a second slot map beside generic_use_slot_bindings, the use site's raw children inserted unresolved, rewriting the node reached through inferred that instantiate_scrutinee_expansion warns binds twice for a generic callee -- and review 95641 refused it. The rework then measured whether any pass was needed at all, with three binaries and controls A/B/C on each: base (no fix) all three red; the mint fix alone, with v1.compiler.infer_patterns exactly as main has it, all three green; the mint fix plus a re-added binding pass, also green. The substitution a generic scrutinee already passes through -- substitute_type_slots_scoped, fed by generic_use_slot_bindings -- rewrites a variant field's Resolved { node } on its way through the coproduct, so a second pass is a path that changes nothing, refused in kind by the same standing (gunbc.design_argument::single_authority). Control C, the generic-callee double-bind, is the control that keeps such a path from coming back. Repair, part three: the mint is JUDGED, not trusted -- kernel_mint_shape_diagnostics now runs kernel_optional_shape_refusal over kernel_optional_type_node() itself under the same diagnostic class, because a hand-built second spelling that nobody checks is exactly how this drifted, and the witness's positive control turns red the moment the mint drifts again.",
    "CONTROL (test.claim.emitter_generic_match_field_binding_witness, hook compile_dag_rust_emit_check which lives in the test home, not in gunbc.roadmap): two generic controls bind Present.value out of an Optional<NonEmptyStr> crossed with `as`, from a parameter-typed scrutinee and from an fn-returned scrutinee -- the distinct acquisition paths the defect was bisected across -- asserting the realized cast and forbidding compile_error!; these are RED on the unfixed seed (the refusal renders compile_error!) and GREEN after the fix. Control C puts the scrutinee behind a GENERIC CALLEE (fn f<T>(x: T) -> Optional<T>) -- the double-bind instantiate_scrutinee_expansion warns about, where a second map would rebind the same slot from the callee's returned type node -- and emits the realized cast with no compile_error!. Control G binds a NON-generic enum field and crosses it with `as`; it emits on both sides and is the negative control pinning the generic-specificity. The mint-side judgment is pinned in test.claim.kernel_optional_mint_shape_witness: a coproduct spelled WITHOUT its type parameter -- the shape the mint had drifted to -- is refused, and the conforming-declaration control there now also fails if the mint itself stops being Optional<T> with Present { value: T }. This row claims NO rung increase: fleet regeneration and execution against the base binary remain pending.",
  ],

  evidence: [],
}
